average is below the long one, so the trend is bearish.\n- **MACD histogram: +34.5**, still below zero on the MACD line but improving. Bearish momentum is fading.\n\n**How to read it:** An RSI of 45.65 means BTC is neither overbought nor oversold. Selling pressure has slightly outweighed buying, but nothing is stretched. RSI alone gives no strong signal here. With a bearish trend and a bullish-turning MACD, the indicators are mixed.\n\n### How to use it\n1. **Mean reversion:** Look for a bounce when RSI is below 30, or a drop when it is above 70. This works best in ranging markets.\n2. **Trend filter:** In strong trends RSI can stay overbought or oversold for a long time. In a downtrend, RSI often stays between 30 and 60, and rallies that fail near 60 can be sell signals.\n3. **Divergence:** If BTC makes a lower low but RSI makes a higher low, selling momentum is weakening and a reversal may be near. This is often the most valuable RSI signal.\n4. **Confirm it:** Pair RSI with MACD, Bollinger Bands or the SMAs. Don't trade on RSI alone.\n\n### Limits\n- RSI is a lagging indicator, so it is calculated from past prices.\n- \"Overbought\" does not mean \"about to fall.\"\n- Shorter timeframes produce more noise. Compare the 15m RSI with the 1h or 4h RSI.\n\nWant me to show BTC on a higher timeframe so you can compare the RSI readings?\n\n*This is educational analysis, not financial advice. Binary options carry significant risk. You can lose your entire stake on any single trade.*"}]